# NP-completeness proof: 3SAT is NP-Complete
# Simplified version that requires only a standard DAG.


exercise_id: https://github.com/CSSPLICE/peml-feasibility-examples/blob/main/parsons/pb-SAT23SAT-DAG.peml

title: Prove that 3-Satisfiability is NP-Complete
author: Cliff Shaffer
license.id: MIT
license.owner.name: Cliff Shaffer
license.owner.email: shaffer@vt.edu

tags.topics: PEML NP-completeness

settings.grader.type: order

instructions:----------
Show a proof that 3-Satisfiability (3SAT) is NP-complete.
----------

[systems]
language: natural

[.assets.code.blocks.content]

blockid: inNP
display: 1) Show that 3SAT is in NP.
depends:

blockid: algorithm
display: Nondeterministically guess truth values for all of the variables. If these truth values yield an equation that is satisfied, then return YES. Otherwise return NO.
depends: inNP

blockid: d1
display: Try all possible combinations of setting truth values for each variable in the expression in turn. If any one of them yields a satified equation, then return YES. Otherwise return NO.
depends: -1

blockid: NPhard
display: 2) Show that 3SAT is NP-hard by using a reduction of known NP-complete problem SAT to 3SAT.
depends: algorithm

blockid: d2
display: 2) Show that 3SAT is NP-hard by using a reduction of 3SAT to known NP-complete problem SAT.
depends: -1

blockid: starthard
display: Show transformations to reduce SAT to 3SAT.
depends: NPhard

blockid: d3
display: Show transformations to reduce 3SAT to SAT.
depends: -1

blockid: dbackwards
display: The transformations are both null operations since any instance of 3SAT is also an instance of SAT.
depends: -1

blockid: t1
display: To reduce a SAT input instance **I** to a 3SAT input instance **I'**, convert each clause of **I** to an equivalent series of clauses that contain exactly 3 literals. We will ignore the long, drawn-out details for how this is done.
depends: starthard

blockid: t2
display: Accept the output **SOL'** from 3SAT on input **I'** as the solution **SOL** for SAT.
depends: t1
[]
[]
